# MOONSHIPS — MEGAPROMPT 3
## M2 Vertical Slice Completion → Verified MVP → Release Candidate

**Repository:** `https://github.com/bjo163/moonships`  
**Canonical branch:** `main`  
**Operating mode:** Autonomous implementation, test-driven execution, evidence-based completion  
**Primary objective:** Turn the current partially implemented M2 foundation into a working, persistent Projects & Tasks desktop workflow, reconcile the backlog, close safely completable blockers, and prepare a verified release candidate.

---

## 0. EXECUTION CONTRACT

Act as the senior engineer, technical lead, QA engineer, release manager, and repository maintainer for MOONSHIPS.

Do not stop after producing a plan. Inspect, implement, test, fix, document, commit, and verify the work until the defined completion boundary is reached.

Do not ask the user to approve every small step. Make reversible, scope-consistent engineering decisions independently. If a genuinely blocking external requirement exists, complete all independent work first and document the exact blocker.

### Mandatory safety rules

- Preserve the existing `main` branch and repository history.
- Never force-push, reset user work, delete untracked user files, or overwrite unrelated changes.
- Never claim that a feature, test, Windows interaction, release, or acceptance criterion passed without evidence.
- Do not mark a task complete merely because its code exists or a commit was created.
- Do not create duplicate GitHub Issues or duplicate task IDs.
- Reuse the canonical backlog, existing milestones, and existing issue synchronization workflow.
- Do not introduce a new framework, database, dependency, or architectural subsystem without a demonstrated need.
- Keep the application local-first. Do not introduce mandatory cloud services, telemetry, Redis, or remote account dependencies.
- Never log secrets or introduce arbitrary AI-driven shell execution, hidden destructive actions, or unsafe filesystem access.
- Preserve existing user data and migration compatibility.
- Keep Windows-first desktop support as a release priority.
- Do not publish a stable release simply because CI passes.

