- Latest commits, workflow runs, and published releases

Known reference state from 2026-10-26, which MUST be revalidated:

- Recent commits include M2-006 activity persistence and M2-008 project update/archive implementation.
- M2-001, M2-002, M2-003, M2-006, M2-007, and M2-009 have been marked complete in the backlog at the last observed checkpoint.
- M2-008 had implementation commits but remained unchecked in the backlog.
- M2-004, M2-005, M2-010 through M2-025, M2-028, M2-031 through M2-035 contain remaining work.
- Latest observed CI run `40250527318` was still running its Windows package smoke-build job. Its final result must be checked before deciding whether it needs a rerun.
- The latest observed published release was `v0.1.0-alpha.2`.

These are historical hints, not assumptions about current state.

### Reconnaissance deliverable

Create a concise evidence matrix:

| Task/area | Current state | Evidence | Missing acceptance criteria | Next action |
|---|---|---|---|---|

Do not create a new branch merely to organize the work. Work on the existing authorized branch and preserve any unrelated changes.

## 2. M2-008 — FINISH PROJECT UPDATE/ARCHIVE

Inspect the implementation introduced by the recent M2-008 commits.

Verify:

- Project updates validate all editable fields.
- Archive is soft-delete/archive behavior, not destructive deletion.
- Workspace/project ownership and relationships are validated.
- Meaningful updates create the correct ActivityEvent.
- Project mutation and its activity event are atomic where required.
- Failed operations do not leave partial records or incorrect activity.
- Archived projects are excluded from ordinary active views and can be recovered if the product contract requires restoration.
- Tests use isolated disposable databases or the established safe test harness.
- Existing migration behavior remains compatible.

Add or correct tests for every missing acceptance criterion.

Run relevant tests and the full repository CI locally where possible.

Only after the implementation and acceptance tests pass:

- Update the canonical backlog.
- Synchronize the corresponding existing GitHub Issue idempotently.
- Record commit/test evidence in the engineering handoff.

## 3. FINISH THE TASK DOMAIN

Inspect the existing Task schema and service. Preserve canonical status and priority values already established by the repository. Do not invent a conflicting second enum or state machine.

Implement or complete the following tasks in dependency order.

### 3.1 M2-010 — Task update service

Acceptance criteria:

- Validate title and supported editable fields.
- Reject unsupported status/priority values.
- Validate referenced projects and workspace ownership.
- Preserve creation metadata.
- Update modification timestamps correctly.
- Create activity records for meaningful changes.
- Failed updates leave the database unchanged.
- Test valid, invalid, unchanged, and missing-record cases.

### 3.2 M2-011 — Archive and restore tasks

Acceptance criteria:

- Archiving does not physically delete the task.
- Restore returns the task to its valid active state.
- Archived tasks can be filtered separately.
- Repeated archive/restore operations have defined, tested behavior.
- Activity history is preserved.
- Tests cover missing tasks, repeat operations, and persistence.

### 3.3 M2-012 — Canonical status transition policy

Acceptance criteria:

- Define one reusable status-transition policy in the domain/service layer.
- All task views call the same policy.
- Invalid transitions are rejected consistently.
- Tests cover allowed transitions, rejected transitions, and unchanged status.
- Document the policy and avoid duplicating it inside UI components.

### 3.4 M2-013 — Due date/time handling

Acceptance criteria:

- Establish an explicit serialization contract.
- Persist timestamps in UTC when the field represents an instant.
- Treat date-only deadlines as date-only values if that matches the existing domain contract; do not accidentally shift them through timezone conversion.
- Display timestamps in local time where appropriate.
- Test round-trip conversion and daylight-saving/timezone boundaries where relevant.
- Never silently reinterpret existing stored dates.

### 3.5 M2-004 — Labels and task-label relationship

Acceptance criteria:

- Labels belong to the appropriate workspace scope.
- Prevent invalid cross-workspace relationships.
- Define case-sensitivity and duplicate-label rules.
- Ensure task-label links are constrained and cleaned up safely.
- Add migration, service, and repository tests.

### 3.6 M2-005 — Task dependencies

Acceptance criteria:

- Prevent self-dependency.
- Prevent duplicate dependency edges.
- Validate task/workspace scope.
- If dependency cycles are forbidden by the product contract, reject cycles and test them.
- Preserve relationships when tasks are archived.
- Do not invent complex dependency scheduling.

### 3.7 M2-031 — Repository integration tests

Use the existing database stack and a disposable test database.

Cover:

- Fresh schema creation and migration upgrade.
- Project create/update/archive.
- Task create/update/archive/restore.
- Status transitions.
- Workspace/project ownership constraints.
- Activity persistence and transactional rollback.
- Labels and dependencies when implemented.
- Isolation from the user's normal application database.

Tests must be deterministic and safe to run repeatedly.

## 4. BUILD THE REAL PROJECTS & TASKS UI

The product is not complete if the backend works but the primary workflow is only a preview, placeholder, or mock.

Inspect the current frontend architecture and implement the smallest coherent vertical slice using the existing framework and component conventions.

Do not rebuild the whole design system or introduce a premium UI package as part of this milestone.

### 4.1 M2-014 — Project list/create/edit

Build a working Projects experience.

Required behavior:

- Load persisted projects.
- Create a project with required validation.
- Edit project details.
- Archive projects without accidental permanent deletion.
- Open a project overview.
- Refresh displayed state after successful mutations.
- Show accurate loading, empty, and error states.
- Persist data across application restart.

### 4.2 M2-015 — Project overview

Show:

- Project name and description/goal.
- Status.
- Actual task count derived from persisted records.
- Recent real activity where available.
- Clear zero-task and archived states.

Do not show fake activity, synthetic counts, or decorative statistics that imply real data.

### 4.3 M2-016 — Task creation

Build a usable task creation form or dialog.

Required fields:

- Title, required.
- Description, optional.
- Project, optional if the domain supports inbox tasks.
- Canonical status and priority.
- Due date only if supported by the current domain model.

Required behavior:

- Accessible validation messages.
- Clear cancel and save actions.
- Escape must not accidentally save.
- Prevent duplicate submissions while a mutation is pending.
- Handle backend errors without losing all user input.
- Focus behavior must be sensible and keyboard accessible.

### 4.4 M2-017 — Task detail

Display and edit supported Task fields.

Required behavior:

- Load the selected persisted task.
- Edit title and description.
- Update status and priority using canonical domain rules.
- Edit due date if supported.
- Archive and restore when appropriate.
- Display meaningful activity history when implemented.
- Show explicit loading, not-found, archived, and error states.
- Do not leak internal errors or secrets into user-visible messages.

### 4.5 M2-018 — Task list

Build a useful List view based on actual database records.

Required behavior:

- Open a task.
- Create a task.
- Display status and priority.
- Support deterministic sorting.
- Refresh after updates.
- Provide empty and error states.
- Work with inbox tasks that have no project.

### 4.6 M2-019 — Kanban

Implement only if the task model and List workflow are stable.

Required behavior:

- Group tasks by the same canonical statuses used in List.
- Changing status persists through the same service.
- Failure restores the previous UI state and shows an understandable error.
- Keyboard-accessible controls must exist; drag-and-drop must not be the only interaction method.

If scope or time prevents a reliable Kanban implementation, document the remaining work instead of shipping a fake or nonfunctional board.

### 4.7 M2-020, M2-021, M2-022

Implement:

- Case-insensitive search across task title and description.
- Combined project/status/priority filters.
- Due-date and priority indicators with text labels, not color alone.
- Clearable search and filter controls.
- Correct empty-results versus loading/error behavior.

### 4.8 M2-025 — Activity history

Show real persisted activity in task details.

Acceptance criteria:

- Relevant task edits and status changes are recorded.
- Events have timestamps and correct entity references.
- History ordering is deterministic.
- No secrets or unnecessary sensitive content are logged.
- Empty history is presented honestly.

### 4.9 M2-028 — Consistent loading, empty, and error states

Audit project and task screens.

Acceptance criteria:

- Loading is not misrepresented as empty.
- Database errors are not silently swallowed.
- Retry is available for safe retriable reads.
- Mutation errors do not imply a successful save.
- Empty states provide a relevant next action.

## 5. COMPLETE THE END-TO-END VERTICAL SLICE

### M2-032 — Required primary acceptance flow

Implement an automated integration or end-to-end test wherever the existing architecture supports it.

The test must verify:

1. Launch with a fresh or disposable test database.
2. Create a project.
3. Confirm the project is persisted.
4. Create a task linked to the project.
5. Confirm the task appears in the project and task list.
6. Update the task status.
7. Confirm the status is reflected consistently in every applicable view.
8. Close and reopen the application or reinitialize the application data layer.
9. Confirm the project, task, status, and relevant activity remain persisted.
10. Confirm invalid input produces a failure rather than silent success.

If native desktop automation is unavailable, implement the strongest reliable service/UI integration test possible and document the remaining manual Windows test separately. Never call a service test a full desktop E2E test.

### M2-033 — Home dashboard

Only implement after the underlying data flow is real.

Show a small, useful dashboard derived from persisted records:

- Recent projects.
- Actual task counts.
- Upcoming/overdue tasks if due dates are supported.
- Recent actual activity.
- Honest first-run and error states.

Do not invent data or add analytics infrastructure.

### M2-034 — Global search

Only after local task search works.

Search supported local entities using the existing command/search architecture. Results must identify entity type, be keyboard accessible, respect archived state and scope, and open the corresponding real record.

Avoid duplicating the existing Command Palette or introducing a second competing global shortcut.

### M2-035 — Task-based usability validation

Prepare a repeatable test script covering:

- Create project.
- Create task.
- Find task.
- Change status.
- Return to work after restart.

Record the test environment, observations, blockers, and follow-up issue IDs.

If actual users or Windows desktop execution are unavailable, mark the manual validation pending. Do not fabricate observations or claim the acceptance criterion passed.

## 6. CLOSE INDEPENDENT M1 ITEMS SAFELY

Inspect and work on independent, implementable M1 items while M2 proceeds.

### M1-016 — Explicit Quit lifecycle

- Explicit Quit terminates the application cleanly.
- Hide-to-tray remains distinct from Quit.
- Stop managed workers/resources where applicable.
- Test lifecycle behavior using the available platform test strategy.

### M1-017 — Single-instance behavior

- Determine the supported Tauri/platform mechanism.
- Prevent confusing duplicate windows or document/test the chosen alternative.
- Ensure the second launch cannot corrupt application state.
- Keep platform-specific assumptions explicit.

### M1-038 — Minimum window and overflow

- Define a justified minimum size.
- Verify layout behavior at 960×640, 1280×720, and 1440×900 where feasible.
- Ensure primary actions remain accessible.
- Document which viewport/scaling checks have and have not been executed on real Windows.

### M1-039 — Visual and accessibility review

- Review keyboard navigation, focus, labels, contrast, reduced motion, and dialogs.
- Measure contrast where relevant against WCAG 2.2 AA criteria.
- Record actual findings and known issues.
- Do not claim accessibility certification based solely on source inspection or automated unit tests.

### M1-040 — Desktop usability

- Verify first launch, primary navigation, Command Palette, profile/layout preferences, and tray behavior.
- Preserve manual verification as pending if no actual Windows environment is available.

### M1-042 — Original application/tray identity

- Create or complete an original Moonships app/tray mark.
- Include necessary icon sizes and asset manifest.
- Record provenance and license status.
- Do not copy third-party brand assets.

### M1-043 and M1-044 — Sound

Do not let optional audio delay the core persistence workflow.

If the sound service is implemented:

- Master sound is off by default.
- No audio plays on first launch.
- Missing audio assets never break operations.
- Only assets with verified provenance/licensing may ship.
- Otherwise retain no-op sound events and document the remaining scope.

### M1-045 — Screenshot review

- Establish a reproducible screenshot capture/review procedure.
- Record commit, OS, scaling, viewport, theme, and flow.
- Do not use screenshots as a substitute for functional or accessibility tests.
- Do not invent screenshots or claim a Windows screenshot was captured if it was not.

## 7. TESTING AND ENGINEERING QUALITY GATES

Inspect the actual project scripts before running commands. Do not assume script names are unchanged.

Run all relevant checks, including the equivalent of:

```bash
npm ci
npm run format:check
npm run lint
npm run test:repo
npm test
npm run typecheck
npm run build
cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml -- --check
cargo test --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --all-targets
cargo clippy --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml --all-targets -- -D warnings
```

If a command does not exist, inspect the actual scripts and run the correct equivalent. Do not add redundant scripts just to make this list pass.

Also verify:

- Migration tests and test database isolation.
- Frontend behavior tests.
- Tauri capabilities and permissions.
- Dependency and lockfile consistency.
- Production frontend build.
- Windows installer smoke-build for configured installer targets.
- Artifact upload and installer metadata where available.

Fix failures caused by your changes. Do not mask failures, weaken meaningful tests, skip acceptance cases, or silently downgrade checks.

If a failure is environmental, capture the exact error and classify it separately.

